Statements:
a statement is a declaritve sentence that either true or false.
there are 2 types of statements:
-atomic statements: consists only of 1 statement
-molecular statements: can be divided into multiple smaller statements.

NOTE: we can make up complicated statements by connecting 2 or more statements (either atomic or molecular) via logical connectives.

-we can say that there are 5 types of logical connectives:
and, or, if then, if and only if, not
-the first 4 are binary connectives and the last one is unary.

NOTE: whether the complicated statement is true or false is determined by the type of connective used and value of each sub statement.

NOTE: when we analyze a complicated statement we don't care about what the statements say, but we only care about whether each statement is a true or a false statement, so we can use propositional variable (sentinal variable) to represent them.
and we also have symbols to represent the connectives discusses above.

The truth table for each logical connective:
P and Q: the final statement is true if both P and Q are true.
P or Q: the final statement is true if one of P or Q or both are true.
if P then Q: the final statement is true when P is False or Q is True or both P is false and Q is true, and the final statement is false when P is true and Q is false.
P if and only if Q: is true when both are true or both are false.
NOT P: is true when P is false.

Logical Equavliance:
2 statements that have the same logical forms and the same truth values then they are logically equavliant.

NOTE: we can use one of 2 methods to prove that 2 statements are not logically equalivant:
1.using a truth table and see where the values differ
2.the other way is to come up with 2 concrete examples for the 2 statements and prove that one is true and the other one is false and hence they are not logically equalivant.

NOTE: logical connectives and and or must be connecting 2 compelete statements in order to apply laws like de morgran's on them.

Tautology:
is a statement form that is always true no matter what the truth value of its individual statements.

Contracdaction:
is a statement form that is always false no matter what the truth value of its individual statements.

Implication:
whenever the hypothisis is false which means that it does not lead or cause the conclusion then the implication is true.
the only case where the implication is false, is when the hypothisis is true and the conclusion is false.

there are 3 other types of implications that we can construct from the original implication:
-converse: switch the propositions q -> p
-inverse: negate the propsotions not p -> not q
-contrapositive: do both (negate and switch) not q -> not p

NOTE: the contrapostive will always have the same truth value as the original implication


if and only if:
is biconditional or 2 way implication and is denoted p <-> q and it's read as p if and only if q.
in order for the biconditional to be true both propositons must be the same.

NOTE: when constructing the truth table of any proposition the no of combination that we have is 2^n where n is the no of propositions that we have.

Order of operations for logical connectives:
Not           -> 1
And           -> 2
Or            -> 3
Implication   -> 4
Biconditional -> 5

NOTE: when constructing a truth table of compound propositions, you mainly have 3 parts: 
-combinations: which is determined by the no of propositions that you have in your compound proposision
-parts: which constitues the final compound proposision and the order of columns relys on the order of operations.
-result: which is the final proposision result.

Classifying Wffs:
Tautlogy: when all of the rows are true
Contradictory: when all of the rows are false
Contingent: at least one true, at least one false.

De Morgan's Laws:
-if you have: not (p and q) = not p or not q
-if you have: not (p or q) = not p and not q

Distributive Laws:
-if you have: p and (q or r) then this will be:
(p and q) or (p and r)

Conditional Law:
-if you have: if p then q you can replace it with:
not p or q


